+# Veterinarian Directory Filters Run in the Database and Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`REQ-VET-003` narrows both forms of the veterinarian directory to one named specialty. The forms are the paged page and the machine-readable list, which `REQ-VET-004` reinstates as supported. The name is caller-supplied text taken from the address.
+
+Every read on `VetRepository` is cached in one cache that has no size limit and no eviction. The narrowed NG-9 admits further filters by shown attributes, so the choice made here becomes the pattern for them.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Filter the cached full list in memory.** The cache stays bounded by the number of veterinarians. But matching and paging would move into the controller, which the *Web controller* row of `architecture-principles.md` forbids. Paging would also diverge from the database paging the directory uses today.
+2. **A repository query per filter, cached by its arguments.** This reuses the existing cache. But every distinct caller-supplied name adds a permanent entry to an unbounded cache, so any client can grow memory without limit.
+3. **A repository query per filter, uncached** (chosen). Matching and paging stay declarative in the repository, as owner search does. Cache growth stays independent of caller input.
+
+## Decision
+
+Each directory filter is a parameterized repository query that pages in the database. It matches the whole stored value, ignoring letter case. A veterinarian is returned once, however many of their values match. Filtered reads carry no cache annotation. Only the unfiltered reads stay cached.
+
+The controller treats an absent or blank filter as no filter and routes it to the cached unfiltered read. It passes any other value on unchanged.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- An unfiltered directory request costs what it did before. A narrowed request always queries the database. The directory is small, so the cost is one join query per request.
+- Cache size stays independent of caller input for filters. The page number is already a caller-chosen cache key on the unfiltered paged read. That exposure predates this decision and is not widened by it.
+- A later filter by another shown attribute follows the same shape: its own query, uncached, blank treated as absent.
+- Caching a filter later would need a bounded, evicting cache first. That depends on Open Question 5 in `system-design.md`.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003, REQ-VET-004
+
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/VetRepository.java`
+- `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/vet/VetController.java`
+- `src/main/resources/templates/vets/vetList.html`
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts): the `VetRepository` and `VetController` rows
+- [Server-Side HTML Rendering with Thymeleaf](2026-07-31-server-side-rendering-with-thymeleaf.md): the machine-readable vet route it records as the one departure
+- [Veterinarian Search Stays Out of Scope, but Filtering by Specialty Is In](2026-09-18-non-goal-veterinarian-search-narrowed.md)
